# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine last saved a file.
#
# The whole tree was once rewritten to CRLF by something on the Windows side.
# Nothing in the repository noticed, because git stores LF and the working tree
# is free to differ — until the day that working tree is committed, and every
# .sh and .command in the project gains a carriage return on its shebang line.
# A shell then reads that return as part of the interpreter's name and the
# launchers stop working on macOS and Linux, which is exactly what the test
# suite reports today.
#
# `* text=auto` normalises text to LF in the repository whatever the checkout
# looks like. The explicit lines below matter more: these are files whose
# meaning depends on their line endings, so they are LF everywhere, including
# in a Windows working tree.

* text=auto

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf

# ...and these are the ones Windows genuinely wants CRLF for.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never touches them.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.onnx      binary
*.xml       -text
